 May 14th, 2008, 03:28 AM #1 Newbie   Joined: May 2008 From: Jolyout Posts: 1 Thanks: 0 Math Studies Hey guys help me with this easy problem please. Find the equation of the line with slope 3 and passing through the point (2,-3). Write your answer in standard form.
 May 14th, 2008, 05:47 AM #2 Senior Member   Joined: Oct 2007 From: Chicago Posts: 1,701 Thanks: 3 This should be in the "algebra, geometry, etc." sub-forum, but: The line has slope 3, which means y = 3x +b, for all values of x and some value of b. So, we plug in our point for x and y: -3 = (3)(-2) + b (you should solve tis for practice before looking below) -3 = -6 + b b = 3 Now, we have the equation: y=3x+3 But, we want it in standard form, so the constant is the only thing on the right side; so, we subtract 3x: y-3x=3 And we're done. Cheers

